
Collections Framework: (part of util)

provides a unified architecture for storing and manipulating groups of objects.
offering interfaces(List, Set, Map, Queue) and implementations (ArrayList, HashMap, HashSet) to manage data efficiency.
it overcomes the array resizing efficiently using ArrayList.

key components:

 Interface => Define core behaviours such as List(Ordered), Set(unique elements), Map(key-value) pair , Queue.
 implementations => actual reusable data structures like ArrayList, LinkedList, HashSet, TreeSet, HashMap, and TreeMap.
 Algorithms => Methods providing sorting and searching functionality, notably within the collections utility class.


core interfaces:

1)collecction(root interface)
    Base interface for all collections

Types under
Collection
    |---- List
    |---- Set
    |---- Queue

2)List (Ordered, Allows Duplicates):
    Maintains insertion order
common classes:
        ArrayList
        LinkedList
        vector

3) Set (No duplicates)
Does Not allow duplicate values

common classes:
        HashSet -> unordered
        LinkedHashSet -> Maintains insertion order
        TreeSet -> sorted order


4) Queue (Fifo - First in First Out)
Used for processing elements in order

common classes:
        PriorityQueue
        LinkedList
        ArrayDeque


3) Map Interface  (key - Value pair)
Not Part of collection (separate hierarchy)

common classes:
        HashMap
        LinkedHashMap
        TreeMap

4)Important Differences

Feature        List         Set          Map

order          yes         Depends      Depends
Duplicates     yes           no         key: no
Index access   yes           no          no


5)common methods

        add()
        remove()
        size()
        contains()
        clear()
        isEmpty()

6) When to use what?

ArrayList => fast access , frequent reads
LinkedList => Frequent insert/delete
Hashset => Unique elements
HashMap => Key-Value lookup(fast)
TreeSet/ TreeMap => Sorted data



